Use super inside __init__ to run the parent's initializer, then add your own setup.
```python
class Parent:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

class Child(Parent):
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.age = age
```
Now Child objects have both name and age.

```python
class Animal:
    def sound(self):
        return "generic sound"

class Dog(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        return "bark"

print(Dog().sound())
```

Yahan Dog class ne Animal ka sound method override kiya, to Dog ka object call karne par "bark" milta hai.

```python
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self._name = name

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self._name

    @name.setter
    def name(self, value):
        self._name = value
```
Ab aap p.name se value le sakte ho aur p.name = 'Rohit' se set kar sakte ho.

Sure! In a class you define both methods. When you print the object, __str__ is used; in the interpreter, __repr__ shows up. Example:
```python
class Point:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x, self.y = x, y
    def __repr__(self):
        return f'Point({self.x}, {self.y})'
    def __str__(self):
        return f'({self.x}, {self.y})'
```
Now print(Point(1,2)) gives (1, 2) while just typing Point(1,2) shows Point(1, 2).

